A sequence of random trials is conducted, and we maintain confidence that the number of successful outcomes will consistently fall within a predetermined range, estimating its lower and upper bounds with increasing accuracy as the number of trials grows. We demonstrate the optimal strategy to achieve the highest probability of our hypothesis remaining unchanged, and reveal that the likelihood of our hypothesis remaining unchanged converges to a specific value as the number of trials approaches infinity. Furthermore, our analysis indicates that the probability of both lower and upper bounds being tight converges to a specific number greater than zero. Lastly, the results of simulated trials conform to our theoretical predictions. 

